    "rules": "Teams must register online at techfest.org and will receive a Team ID for future reference.\r\nRegistration is open until 15th October and Round 1 submissions are open until 17th October 2026.\r\nSolutions must be original and must not be protected by existing patents, copyrights, or publications.\r\nThe decision of the organizers and judges shall be treated as final and binding on all. Techfest reserves the right to verify participant details submitted by teams.\r\nNo responsibility will be held by Techfest, IIT Bombay for any late, lost, or misdirected entries.\r\nNote that at any point in time the latest information will be that which is on the website; registered participants will be informed through mail about any changes.\r\nAll official communication will be through the Techfest email (satwik@techfest.org); participants are advised to keep track of all folders in their e-mail account.",
    "faq": "Q: Who can participate?\r\nA:The competition is open to all students with a valid ID from their educational institution. Cross-institutional teams are encouraged.\r\n \r\nQ: What's the team size?\r\nA:Maximum 4 members per team.\r\n \r\nQ: What's the difference between Round 1 and Round 2?\r\nA:Round 1 is a written abstract (team info, problem statement, background, proposed solution and material design, and impact/scalability) submitted by 17th October. Round 2 is a comprehensive final report plus evidence of a working prototype, submitted by 5th December, followed by an on-site presentation at Techfest, IIT Bombay.\r\n \r\nQ: Do we need a working prototype?\r\nA:Yes, for Round 2 teams must provide evidence of a working prototype and its functional performance during its operating life, not just a design proposal.\r\n \r\nQ: How do we submit our abstract and final report?\r\nA:By emailing them to compi@techfest.org with the subject line \"\"Ideate Submission - [Team ID] - [Round Name].\r\n \r\nQ: What application areas can we choose?\r\nA:Any domain where a transient or low-impact device adds value, healthcare, environmental monitoring, agriculture, wearables, packaging, or others. Each solution must clearly justify why a biodegradable approach suits it better than a conventional one.\r\n \r\nQ: What do we need to specify about degradation?\r\nA:The expected operational lifespan of the device and the environmental conditions under which it is designed to degrade (soil, water, compost, or human body).\r\n \r\nQ: How is the competition judged?\r\nA:On functional performance, lifecycle sustainability, degradation feasibility, practical relevance of the application, and the rigor of benchmarking and design justification against existing alternatives.\r\n \r\nQ: What are the key dates?\r\nA:Registration closes 5th October, the abstract is due 17th October, shortlisting is announced 10th November, the final report is due 5th December, and finals run 15th-18th December 2026.\r\n \r\nQ: What certificates are awarded?\r\nA:The top three teams in Round 2 receive a Certificate of Excellence; all teams that qualify for Round 2 receive an E-certificate.\r\n \r\nQ: How is prize money disbursed?\r\nA:To the top three teams via NEFT, by emailing account details and a passbook photograph to satwik@techfest.org immediately after results are announced.",
    "contact": "satwik@techfest.org\r\nSatwik Patti\r\n7842625477",
