    "compi_id": "roboreach",
    "name": "RoboReach",
    "genre_display": "Zonals",
    "prize": "INR 1,00,000",
    "desc": "A hardware challenge where students build a manually controlled wireless bot with a mounted robotic arm and guide it through a 13-task arena testing precision grasping, stability, and control.",
    "compiImg": "https://techfest.org/media/compiImg/image-clean_2_1.webp",
    "probStatement": "https://techfest.org/media/compiProbStatement/RoboReach_compressed.pdf",
    "max_team_size": 4,
    "about": "RoboReach is Techfest, IIT Bombay's robotic arm competition, Asia's largest science and technology festival, that challenges teams to build a manually controlled, wireless bot with a mounted robotic arm capable of grasping, lifting, stacking, and precisely placing objects while the bot itself navigates ramps, bridges, and tight turns. Across 13 tasks, from picking up a cube and climbing a 15-degree ramp to writing letters with a marker and solving a physical Tower of Hanoi, the arm and bot must work in tandem under a single operator's control. Bring your arm to life at Techfest, IIT Bombay, Asia's largest science and technology festival.",
    "structure": "The competition is held as a qualifying round followed by the Grand Finale at Techfest, IIT Bombay.\r\n \r\n Eligibility: Open to all students with a valid ID from their educational institution. Cross-institutional teams are allowed.\r\n \r\n Team size: Maximum 4 members per team.\r\n \r\n Qualifying Round\r\n Teams bring their wireless bot with a mounted robotic arm to compete on the 13-task arena (Pickup, Ascent, Bridge, Descent Breakdown, Touchdown, Stacker, Longshot, Robonardo DaVinci, Hanoi'd, Buzzed, Diagonals, Escape Room, and The End) in a single 7-minute run, aiming for the top score across 910 possible points plus a time bonus.\r\n \r\n Grand Finale\r\n The top 5 teams advance to the Grand Finale at Techfest, IIT Bombay.",
    "timeline": "Registration closes - As per the date of respective city zonal\r\n\r\nRound 1 attempt  - On the date of that city zonal\r\n\r\nZonal results & shortlist announcement - On the date of that city zonal\r\n\r\nRound 2: Finals at Techfest, IIT Bombay - 16th-18th december 2026",
    "rules": "Every team must register online at techfest.org and will receive a Team ID for future reference.\r\nOnly wireless bots are allowed; the SSID/device name must follow TeamName_TF (first 20 characters if longer), and each robot must implement an authentication mechanism restricting control to the team's own controller.\r\nObjects must be grasped using a mechanical gripper only; adhesives, magnets, hooks, and suction cups are prohibited.\r\nParticipants may not use readymade Lego components; readymade gear assemblies are allowed.\r\nProjectiles, flames, explosives, compressed gas weapons, liquids, adhesives, and entanglement devices are prohibited.\r\nAny damage to the arena leads to immediate disqualification.\r\nThe decision of the organizers shall be treated as final and binding on all disputes and discrepancies.\r\nThe latest information will be that which is on the website; registered participants will be informed through mail about any changes.",
    "faq": "Q: Who can participate?\r\nA: Open to all students with a valid ID from their educational institution. Cross-institutional teams are allowed.\r\n \r\n Q: What's the team size?\r\n A: Maximum 4 members per team, and only one person may control the bot at any point in time.\r\n \r\n Q: What are the bot and arm specifications?\r\n A: The bot must fit in a 300mm x 200mm x 300mm box, move on wheels in continuous ground contact, and carry a robotic arm that can grasp objects at a height of 450mm using only a mechanical gripper (no adhesives, magnets, hooks, or suction cups). It must be controlled wirelessly (WiFi or Bluetooth) with an authentication mechanism restricting control to the team's own controller.\r\n \r\n Q: What happens if the bot disconnects from the joystick mid-run?\r\nA:  Its motors must stop, arm motion must cease, and the gripper must hold its last position until a new valid command is received. One team member may then handle the bot after communicating with the organizers, with no repositioning or repairs allowed; there's no penalty, but the clock keeps running.\r\n \r\n Q: How many runs do we get?\r\n A: Each team gets one run, up to 7 minutes long, to maximize their score across all 13 tasks.\r\n \r\n Q: Can we skip tasks?\r\n A: Yes, but no points are awarded for skipped tasks, and skipping may earn a time bonus if you finish under 5 minutes.\r\n \r\n Q: How is the competition scored?\r\n A: Total Score = A (points from completed tasks, max 910) + T (time bonus, max 100) - P (penalties).\r\n \r\n Q: What counts as a penalty?\r\n A: Going out of bounds incurs 10 points per violation; dropping the cube before the Touchdown task incurs 15 points per drop; touching the wire in the Buzzed task incurs 20 points per violation, among other task-specific penalties.\r\n \r\n Q: How do we qualify for the Grand Finale?\r\nA:  The top 5 teams from the qualifying round advance to the Grand Finale on 18th December.",
